6.2 Formal notation. Let C be a claim, a tuple C = ⟨id, π, e, a, t, L, s, d, o⟩, where π is the proposition (a falsifiable sentence), e the event type, a the attributed actor(s), t the temporal specification, L ⊆ {A,…,H} the layer(s) the claim concerns, s the source set, d the dependency set (international and precursor), and o the outcome specification. A claim is well-formed if π is a single falsifiable sentence and |L| ≥ 1. A claim is sourced if s ≠ ∅ and every source has a locator. A claim is audit-complete only when it meets the criteria of Chapter 25.
6.3 Structural rules. E1 is met by section 6.2 well-formedness and section 21 splitting (distinct milestones become distinct claims). E2 by the section 7.2 source fields and the section 9 grid. E3 by the bounded-actor requirement and the section 8.4 country-attribution rule. E4 by the section 4 frame and the section 11 outcome fields. E5 by the section 10.5 decomposition rule.
6.4 Components and splitting (new in v1.2). A claim whose proposition bundles dated events may declare components, each evaluated separately; the claim's effective support is that of its weakest component (section 8.2). Declaring components is a temporary device. Where a bundle also mixes an act with its measured effect, or a historical fact with an interpretive relation (for example, "developed independently"), the claim is split instead, because the components then differ in kind of evidence and not merely in number of sources. v1.2 splits US-NET-001 and US-GPS-004 on this ground (section 21). Two bundles remain and are queued for splitting in the Phase 1 follow-up: US-EL-001 (declared components demonstration and publication) and US-EL-003 (process development and commercial introduction; all its dates come from one lineage, so its k_eff is unaffected).
6.5 Theoretical proposition. P1. National contribution is best represented as a bounded set of evidence-linked roles across a layered process, not as an exclusive ownership label attached to an outcome. P1 is a methodological hypothesis evaluated by the pilots, not an empirical claim about any nation.
7. Methodology: Ledger, Register and Relation Table
7.1 The three instruments.
Instrument 1, Claim Ledger. Fields: claim ID; previous ID (for splits); version; pilot; exact falsifiable claim; event type; declared components; conception, demonstration, public-disclosure, patent-filing, patent-grant, commercial-production and mass-adoption dates; chronology confidence (CC); individuals; institutions; funding; contribution type; precise US contribution; country-applicable flag; state-act flag; international dependencies and whether they have been tested; primary sources; independent corroboration; alternative-explanation or disconfirming test and its status (untested, passed, passed-adversarial, failed); global adoption evidence; human outcomes; costs and risks; attribution confidence (AC) and AC_country; impact evidence (IE); methodological quality (MQ), population (POP), distributional profile (DIST), causal uncertainty (CU); open questions; revision log; research status.
Instrument 2, Source Register. Fields: source ID; title; author or institution; publication or event date; source type; stable URL, DOI or archive ID; page, section or locator; exact quotation; what it establishes; what it does not establish; lineage; archival proximity (P); institutional interest (I); locator precision (L); access date; verification status; notes. Direct relevance (R) is recorded per link, not per source, because it depends on the proposition.
Instrument 3, Relation Table. Fields: link ID; claim ID; source ID; relationship (supports, contradicts, qualifies, contextualizes, duplicate-archive); component addressed; specific proposition; locator; independence group (derived from the source's lineage); probative profile (R, P, I, L); whether the source corroborates the date from an independent lineage; confidence contribution; notes.
7.2 The evidentiary separation principle. A source is evaluated for a specific proposition, not globally. A patent is strong evidence that a filing was made and claims asserted; it is not evidence of priority, successful implementation, commercial success or benefit. A contemporaneous announcement accurately records what was said; it does not verify the technical claim. A later scholarly study may be stronger on chronology where it critically evaluates notebooks, correspondence and patents. The register therefore requires "what it establishes" and "what it does not establish" as mandatory, source-specific fields, and the relation table assigns each source to a specific proposition.
7.3 Event-specific chronology. For each technology or program the ledger separates: precursor work; conception; working demonstration; public disclosure; patent filing; patent grant; engineering or manufacturing milestone; commercial production; standardization or policy change; adoption and human outcomes. Missing evidence is recorded as "not established" (the record does not justify a date), not as "not applicable," which is reserved for fields that genuinely do not apply.
7.4 Relation-table discipline. A list of URLs in a claim row is a database error: it does not specify which sentence each source supports. The relation table makes each source, sentence and lineage assignment explicit and permits one source to bear on several claims, or several components, with different evidentiary roles.
8. The Confidence Calculus (corrected)
This chapter replaces the v1.1 calculus. It resolves four defects: the second cap never bound; the chronology scale defined a value the cap forbade; the attribution scale contained a value no claim could reach; and the country-attribution rule was stated but not applied.
8.1 Definitions.
• k is the number of distinct independence lineages among a claim's links whose relationship is supports and whose direct relevance to the proposition is R ≥ 2. Contextual (R = 1), qualifying, contradicting and duplicate-archive links never raise k.
• k_eff is the minimum of k over the claim's declared components (section 6.4); for a claim without components, k_eff = k. A claim cannot be stronger than its weakest essential component.
• A raw score records what the type of evidence would support. The cap records what the independence of the evidence permits. The reported score is the lesser of the two.
