8.3 Rules of use.
1. R1. Every populated score carries a one-line rationale in the audit record.
2. R2. Scores are recorded per claim, not per pilot.
3. R3. A claim may carry CC = 3 and IE = 1 together. Mixed profiles are normal and are the point.
4. R4. "Not established" is a research task; "not applicable" is a field exclusion. Neither is evidence for or against the claim.
5. R5. CC = 1 or AC = 1 resulting from k_eff = 0 is labelled Indeterminate in the audit record and must not be cited in any summary as a weak positive finding.
6. R6. The script of Appendix N refuses to pass any stored score above its cap, or any AC above 2 while the disconfirming test is untested.
8.4 Country attribution. Attribution to a bounded actor and attribution to a country are distinct. For any claim whose "precise US contribution" asserts a US origin, the ledger reports a second value, AC_country, beside AC:
• AC_country = max(1, AC − 1) while the international-dependency field is untested.
• AC_country = AC once the dependency field has been tested.
• State-act exemption. Where the bounded actor is the national government and the proposition is that government's own act (a program authorization, a policy decision), country and actor coincide, and AC_country = AC. The dependency question then bears on the effect of the act, which is an impact claim scored under section 11, not on who performed it.
• Where the claim asserts no US contribution (non-US origin claims, distributed or multinational claims) AC_country is not applicable.
An AC_country of 1 produced by this rule alone, with AC > 1, is labelled dependency-untested, not Indeterminate: the actor-level attribution is supported; the country-level step has not been tested. v1.1 stated the cap but reported AC only, so the cap never appeared in any score.
8.5 Anti-inflation rule. No claim may carry CC ≥ 3 or AC ≥ 3 on the strength of a single source lineage. This is the k_eff = 1 row of the cap table and is enforced by it, not by a separate rule.
