Source type is metadata and does not determine W. R is recorded per link because the same source can be strong for one proposition and contextual for another. P, I and L are recorded on the link as well, since a source may be consulted at different precision for different propositions.
9.2 Adjudication. Conflict between sources is resolved by comparing profiles dimension by dimension, never by a global ranking.
1. T1. Direct relevance dominates for the proposition at issue: R = 3 outranks R = 1 even if the latter has higher P.
2. T2. Higher P breaks ties at equal R, unless the higher-P source has I = +1 and the lower-P source I = 0 or −1, in which case locator precision and interest are weighed explicitly and the reasoning recorded.
3. T3. Higher L breaks ties at equal R and P.
4. T4. A distinct lineage outranks a derivative one at equal R, P and L.
5. T5. No dimension is decisive alone. Where the comparison yields no clear precedence, the conflict is recorded as unresolved and both sources are retained with their profiles. Unresolved conflicts are findings, not failures.
9.3 Conflict and qualifier. Conflict exists when two sources disagree on a proposition, a date, an attribution or an outcome. Where sources differ in scope rather than contradict, the difference is recorded as a qualifier. A current example: SRC-NET-001 dates the Davies concept to 1965 and SRC-NET-004 dates the proposal to 1966. These differ in event (concept versus proposal), so they are recorded as a qualifier on US-NET-001b and not as a conflict, pending capture of both passages.
9.4 Disconfirming-evidence rule. Every claim records at least one disconfirming test, a question whose affirmative answer would weaken the claim, together with its status: untested, passed, passed-adversarial or failed. Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence; incomplete archives are limitations, not support. The status feeds the attribution scale directly (section 8.1): an untested or failed test holds AC at 2.
9.5 Synthesis-claim decomposition (E5). A synthesis claim (for example, "Internet commercialization resulted from interacting technical, funding, policy and market processes") cannot be verified as a whole. It is decomposed into at least three component causal claims, each independently testable, before any component is scored above IE = 1. Synthesis claims are flagged in the ledger with a decomposition plan.
10. Adjudication, Disconfirming Evidence and Synthesis
10.1 Procedure. When sources conflict, the grid of section 9.2 is applied and the relation table records each source's profile for the disputed proposition. The outcome is one of: resolved in favor of source A (profile comparison recorded), resolved in favor of source B, or unresolved (both retained).
10.2 Tie-breaking. As section 9.2, T1 to T5.
10.3 Conflict and qualifier. As section 9.3.
10.4 Disconfirming-evidence rule. As section 9.4. The test and its status are recorded in the ledger's disconfirming-test field. A claim whose test is untested cannot exceed AC = 2; AC = 3 requires a passed test and AC = 4 an adversarial one. A claim cannot satisfy this rule by asserting that its own wording already encodes the test: a proposition that states independence ("developed independently") is itself a claim requiring sources, not a passed test of the claims it qualifies. This is the correction to the v1.1 treatment of US-NET-001.
10.5 Synthesis-claim decomposition. As section 9.5. The decomposition plan is recorded in the ledger's open-questions field with at least three component claims specified.
11. Human Outcome Measurement
A causal study is not robust merely because it uses a counterfactual; methodological quality, population, distribution and causal uncertainty are recorded separately.
11.1 The descriptive indicator. IE is defined in section 8.1. It indicates the type of outcome evidence, not its quality.
11.2 Quality and distribution fields. Four fields are recorded for every claim with IE ≥ 2.
• MQ, methodological quality (1 to 4). 1 anecdotal or undocumented; 2 descriptive statistics or case report without an identification strategy; 3 observational study with a stated identification strategy and acknowledged limitations; 4 quasi-experimental or experimental design with an explicit counterfactual, bias assessment and external-validity discussion.
• POP, population specification. The population(s) for which the outcome is evidenced and the limits of generalization. "Global" is not a population specification; "users of consumer GPS receivers in OECD countries, 2005 to 2015" is.
• DIST, distributional profile. Documented inequality in access, benefit or harm across the specified population. Where distribution is unmeasured the field records "unmeasured" and the claim cannot be scored as a universal benefit.
• CU, causal uncertainty. The principal threats to causal inference: omitted variables, selection, reverse causation, measurement error, external-validity limits. Where unassessed the field records "unassessed."
For an outcome claim still at IE < 2 the same fields are filled as a pre-specification of the intended measurement (target population, distributional data to collect, threats to address), marked pending. A pre-specification is a plan, not a score.
11.3 Reporting rule. Effective impact strength is reported as the profile ⟨IE, MQ, POP, DIST, CU⟩, never as one number. IE = 4 with MQ = 1 is reported as a causal design of low methodological quality. IE = 3 with MQ = 4 and DIST unmeasured is reported as a measured outcome of high quality with unmeasured distribution, and cannot be cited as evidence of universal benefit.
11.4 Anti-universalization rule. A favorable outcome result does not become proof of universal benefit. DIST must be populated before any outcome is described as a benefit "for humanity." Where DIST is unmeasured, the claim is described as a benefit for the specified population, with the distributional limit stated.
12. Reproducibility Protocol
This protocol is a pre-registered test designed to give the framework a real chance to fail. The 80% threshold and the one-band tolerance are design choices, not validated thresholds, and are presented as hypotheses.
12.1 Definition. A claim is reproduced when an independent reviewer, given only the source packet (the claim text, the source-register entries for its linked sources, and the relation-table links, without the narrative chapter), can (i) locate every cited source; (ii) verify every quotation against the locator; (iii) assign CC, AC, IE and the section 11.2 fields independently; (iv) state at least one alternative explanation; and reaches scores that differ from the original by at most one band on each ordinal dimension.
12.2 Pre-registered hypotheses.
1. H1. With at least two independent reviewers, at least 80% of reviewer-dimension pairs fall within one band of the original.
2. H2. At least one reviewer independently raises the same alternative explanation recorded in the ledger.
3. H3. Disagreements concentrate in AC and in the section 11.2 fields, not in CC.
These hypotheses may fail. A failure of H1, H2 or H3 is a finding about the framework, not a failure of the reviewers. Lineage assignment is an input to k and is not itself tested by H1 to H3; it is listed as an open item for the Phase 2 audit, because a wrongly merged or split lineage moves the cap by a full band.
12.3 Procedure.
1. Select at least three claims spanning at least two pilots, including at least one claim with k_eff = 1 and one with k_eff ≥ 2.
2. Recruit at least two independent reviewers without access to the narrative.
3. Reviewers complete a blind audit form covering CC, AC, IE, MQ, POP, DIST, CU and alternative explanations.
4. Compute per-dimension agreement and per-claim agreement.
5. Report disagreements by dimension, not only as global pass or fail.
6. Report non-reproducible cases in full, with the reviewers' reasoning.
7. Record any protocol modification made after pre-registration, with the reason.
12.4 Reporting and consequences. The report states, per claim: pass or fail on each hypothesis, band deviations by dimension, alternative explanations raised, and archival gaps identified. A claim that fails is weakened and the weakening is logged under revision control. The report is published alongside the framework, including failures.
12.5 Pre-registration requirement. The protocol is deposited in a public registry before the review is run, recording the claims selected, criteria H1 to H3, the analysis plan and the date. This prevents post-hoc adjustment of thresholds to manufacture a pass.
