14. Pilot I: Transistor and Integrated-Circuit Lineage
The semiconductor pilot tests whether the framework distinguishes a sequence of related but non-identical contributions. It holds 6 claims, 6 sources and 4 lineages.
Claims. The point-contact transistor demonstrated by Bardeen and Brattain at Bell Laboratories in December 1947 and described in a 1948 publication (US-EL-001) is a distinct event from the 1951 junction-transistor paper of Shockley, Sparks and Teal (US-EL-002), a distinct architecture and stage. The integrated-circuit history is split: Kilby's 1958 solid-circuit demonstration (US-EL-005a) and Noyce's 1959 monolithic patent filing (US-EL-005b) are complementary, not interchangeable. Manufacturability is represented by Hoerni's planar process (US-EL-003) and Lehovec's isolation approach (US-EL-004), which are substantive contributions to Layer D, not footnotes to a singular inventor story.
Initial proposition (P-I). The emergence of practical semiconductor technology is better represented as a network of differentiated contributions than as a single invention attributed to one person or nation.
Current evidentiary state. All six claims have supporting links, from four lineages (APS, CHM Silicon Engine, the Bardeen paper, the Shockley paper). Four claims (US-EL-003, -004, -005a, -005b) rest on the single CHM lineage. US-EL-002 rests on the Shockley paper alone. US-EL-001 has two supporting lineages in total (k = 2) but one per component: the demonstration date rests on the APS synthesis and the publication on the Bardeen paper, so k_eff = 1. Every claim therefore carries C_max = 2, CC = 2 and AC = 2, and every AC_country is 1 (dependency-untested). All are IE = 1. The three patents (US 3,025,589; US 3,029,366; US 2,981,877) are in the source map but not yet in the register; the two original papers are now registered.
Open questions. Which dates denote conception and which demonstration; how patent claims align with working devices; precursor genealogy (including the oxide-masking work of Frosch and Derick); alternative manufacturing paths; adoption speed across firms and countries; global diffusion and human outcomes.
15. Pilot II: The Internet Ecosystem
The Internet pilot tests category boundaries and independent development. It holds 8 claims, 4 sources and 4 lineages.
Claims. Packet switching is associated with Baran at RAND and Davies at the UK NPL. v1.1 held both, and their independence, in one claim. v1.2 splits it into three: US-NET-001a (Baran's 1964 RAND reports), US-NET-001b (Davies's work at the NPL, documented in a 1966 proposal) and US-NET-001c (the two were developed independently, so neither national line is the exclusive origin). ARPANET (US-NET-002) is a funded deployment and research-infrastructure milestone, not the Internet. Cerf and Kahn's 1974 paper (US-NET-003) is a protocol proposal; it does not establish deployment or sole authorship. NSFNET (US-NET-004) requires dated backbone and international records. The Web (US-NET-006) is explicitly non-US in origin (CERN), with US networking infrastructure as enabling context. The commercialization synthesis (US-NET-005) is a meta-claim subject to the decomposition rule of section 9.5.
Initial proposition (P-II). The Internet's emergence and diffusion cannot be explained by one invention or institution; they require a documented account of interacting technical, institutional, funding, standards, policy and market processes. This is a causal synthesis and is not verified merely because it sounds balanced.
Current evidentiary state. Three claims have support. US-NET-001a and US-NET-003 each rest on one primary record (k = 1, CC = 2, AC = 2). US-NET-001b has two lineages (the Davies proposal and the NPL history), giving C_max = 4 and CC = 3, but AC is held at 2 because its disconfirming test is untested. The NPL history may derive from Davies's own account, so the independence of that second lineage is a Phase 2 question. US-NET-001c, -002, -004, -005 and -006 have no supporting source: C_max = 1, CC = 1, AC = 1, recorded as Indeterminate. In particular, no register source addresses whether Baran and Davies worked independently; SRC-NET-001 and SRC-NET-004 each state that they do not resolve it.
Open questions. Relative timing and influence of the two packet-switching lines; reconciliation of the 1965 and 1966 dates; the research-network to internetworking transition; international standards and non-US networks; NSFNET's precise scope; commercialization and Web effects on access.
16. Pilot III: Global Positioning System
GPS tests the distinction between creating a technical capability and delivering civilian accessibility. It holds 9 claims, 5 sources and 2 lineages.
Claims and splitting. The precursor bundle is split into Transit (US-GPS-001a), TIMATION (US-GPS-001b), Project 621B (US-GPS-001c) and formal program integration in 1973 (US-GPS-001d, formerly US-GPS-003). Precision timing (US-GPS-002) is an enabling element, not the invention of atomic clocks. v1.1's single Selective Availability claim bundled an act with its effect and is split: US-GPS-004a (the US government discontinued Selective Availability on 1 May 2000) is a policy act; US-GPS-004b (measured civilian accuracy improved afterwards) is an outcome claim. Global adoption (US-GPS-005) and dependency and risk (US-GPS-006) are separately scoped.
Initial proposition (P-III). The civilian value of a navigation system depends not only on engineering capability but on policy, access, receiver technology, complementary infrastructure and resilience.
Current evidentiary state. US-GPS-004a is the strongest dated claim in the dataset. Official GPS.gov and White House records fix the date, but its three linked records share one policy lineage (the archived copy adds nothing), and the FAA and Smithsonian pages are contextual (R = 1). So k_eff = 1, C_max = 2, CC_raw = 3 and CC = 2, AC = 2. Its AC_country is 2 under the state-act exemption of section 8.4. Its impact indicator is IE = 1 (a policy in force). US-GPS-004b, the claim carrying the accuracy effect, has no source: IE = 0, CC = 1, AC = 1, Indeterminate, with its measurement design pre-specified under section 11.2. All other GPS claims have no linked source and are recorded as Indeterminate.
Open questions. Precursor chronologies; clock type by satellite generation; international metrology dependence; quantified accuracy before and after Selective Availability with MQ, POP, DIST and CU populated; sector and country adoption; incident evidence distinguishing jamming from spoofing.
17. Pilot IV: Smallpox Eradication
Smallpox is the stress test because the outcome cannot plausibly be owned by one country or one scientist. It holds 6 claims, 1 source and 1 lineage, and tests whether the ledger preserves distributed agency and differentiated credit while still identifying the precise contributions of US institutions and personnel.
Minimal claim set.
• US-SP-001 The Soviet Union proposed a WHO smallpox eradication program in 1958 (non-US origin; anchor claim).
• US-SP-002 WHO's 1966 intensified program adopted surveillance-containment alongside mass vaccination.
• US-SP-003 US CDC field personnel and D. A. Henderson (program lead from 1966) contributed to WHO coordination and field operations.
• US-SP-004 US vaccine production and supply contributed to the global vaccine stock.
• US-SP-005 National programs and local health teams performed vaccination, surveillance and case containment.
• US-SP-006 Smallpox was certified eradicated in 1980 (outcome claim; IE only after decomposition under section 9.5).
Initial proposition (P-IV). The framework can represent a multinational outcome with differentiated, bounded contributions and without converting a global achievement into a US-centered narrative.
Current evidentiary state. One source exists, the Henderson interview (SRC-SMALLPOX-001). It directly supports the 1958 Soviet proposal and the 1966 surveillance-containment framing (US-SP-001, -002: k = 1, CC = 2, AC = 2) and is contextual only for the 1980 certification. It does not address national shares, vaccine quantities or local-team roles. US-SP-003 to -006 have no supporting source and are Indeterminate. Three relation-table links exist, not six. This pilot is designed and specified but not instantiated; it is the principal empirical task of Phase 6.
Open questions. Relative causal weight of vaccination and surveillance-containment; US and other national contributions; local knowledge and logistics; limits of retrospective attribution; which body's act the 1980 date refers to (a commission finding or an Assembly declaration), which US-SP-006 must specify before scoring; the register does not say.
18. Cross-Pilot Synthesis
Three distinctions recur and constitute the framework's demonstrated payoff.
• S1. Existence is not manufacturability. The semiconductor pilot separates early devices from the processes that made reliable production possible (Layer C versus Layer D).
• S2. A network milestone is not the Internet. Packet switching, ARPANET, TCP/IP, NSFNET, the Web, commercialization and global adoption are related but non-identical objects of explanation (Layers C to G), and "developed independently" is a claim in its own right.
• S3. Capability is not accessibility is not outcome. GPS shows that policy can materially alter the civilian value of a deployed system (Layers F to H), that a policy act and its measured effect are different claims, and that availability is not benefit.
A fourth, meta-level finding: across all pilots, international precursors and parallel development are causal, not decorative. The dependency field and the country-attribution rule (section 8.4) force this into the record rather than leaving it as a caveat.
A fifth finding concerns the dataset itself. Of 29 claims, 12 have at least one supporting source and 17 have none. By pilot the split is semiconductor 6 and 0, Internet 3 and 5, GPS 1 and 8, smallpox 2 and 4. No claim exceeds AC = 2, because no disconfirming test has been run, and only US-NET-001b reaches CC = 3, because it is the only claim with both a primary record and a second independent lineage. These are findings about the state of the evidence, not defects of the method.
The synthesis conclusion is provisional: the method appears to expose category errors and to make claims auditable, but its performance must be judged by the pre-registered reproducibility protocol (Chapter 12), which has not yet been run.
