23. Limitations
1. Theoretical, not empirical. No pilot is fully audited; source packets are incomplete; no reproducibility review has been run.
2. Purposive sampling. The four pilots are chosen for reach and dispute, not representativeness.
3. Pending quotations. Every quotation and exact locator is a placeholder pending archival capture; this is disclosed rather than disguised.
4. Interpretive residue. Records can be missing, strategically produced or unevenly preserved. The ledger improves transparency but cannot eliminate uncertainty.
5. AI-assisted risk. AI drafting can produce confident synthesis unsupported by documents. Mitigations: source-level verification, no AI consensus as corroboration, human accountability. The v1.1 audit that found the defects of this version was itself AI-assisted and is subject to the same limit.
6. Normative contestability. The capability frame of Chapter 4 is defensible but not the only standard; alternative frames would change outcome scoring, not the ledger structure.
7. Unvalidated thresholds. The 80% and one-band criteria of Chapter 12 are hypotheses, not validated thresholds.
8. Unvalidated calculus constants. The cap values (1, 2, 4), the choice that two lineages open the full range, and the ordinal scales are design decisions, not measured quantities. Lineage assignment is expert judgment and moves the cap by a full band when wrong.
9. Remaining bundles. US-EL-001 and US-EL-003 still bundle dated events (section 6.4).
24. Ethics and Research Integrity
The study avoids nationalist, anti-nationalist and presentist distortion. It credits individuals without erasing teams, institutions, public investment, international dependencies or local users. It reports harms and unequal access alongside benefits, without assuming either cancels the other.
No universal contribution score is produced. A single number would conceal the distinction between discovery, engineering, institutional support, access and outcome. Where comparisons occur, dimensions and evidence remain visible.
AI systems may assist with search, extraction, drafting, translation and adversarial critique. Every consequential factual assertion must be traceable to a source a human can inspect. AI-generated consensus is never described as independent corroboration, and no claim is labeled "verified" because it appears in multiple generated drafts. The human research lead retains final interpretive responsibility.
Dual-use and risk disclosure: the GPS dependency claim and any infrastructure-risk findings are reported in a way that does not expose operational vulnerabilities beyond what is already public in technical literature and government advisories.
25. Audit and Completion Criteria
A claim is not complete because its cells contain text. It is audit-complete only when all of the following hold.
1. AC-1 The claim is one falsifiable sentence with a bounded actor and scope.
2. AC-2 Distinct milestones are separated, or declared as components with a splitting plan (no E1 collapse).
3. AC-3 Every populated date has a source with a locator; unpopulated fields read "not established" or "not applicable."
4. AC-4 Every source records "what it establishes" and "what it does not establish."
5. AC-5 Every link carries a probative profile (section 9.1); adjudication outcomes are recorded.
6. AC-6 Lineages are assigned; k and k_eff are computed by script; the cap of section 8.2 is applied and recorded.
7. AC-7 CC, AC, AC_country (where applicable), IE and the section 11.2 fields are scored with rationale; k_eff = 0 claims are labelled Indeterminate.
8. AC-8 At least one disconfirming test is recorded with its status (section 9.4).
9. AC-9 International dependencies are populated or marked "not established," and their test status is recorded (section 8.4).
10. AC-10 Synthesis claims are decomposed or flagged (section 9.5).
11. AC-11 Capability, diffusion, access, outcome and risk are distinguished; DIST is populated before any outcome is described as a universal benefit (section 11.4).
12. AC-12 An independent reviewer can reproduce the claim from the source packet within one band, or the failure is reported (Chapter 12).
13. AC-13 The revision log records every change, including corrections of prior scores.
A "pass" means the procedure is reproducible at the stated scope, not that every historical controversy is settled. Missing evidence remains visible as a research task.
