APPENDIX F — CONFIDENCE CALCULUS: FORMAL RULES AND WORKED COMPUTATIONS

F.1 Definitions (v1.2)

· CC_raw records what the type of evidence would support (0–4).
· AC_raw records what the type of evidence would support (0–4).
· C_max(k_eff) = 1 if k_eff = 0; 2 if k_eff = 1; 4 if k_eff ≥ 2.
· CC = min(CC_raw, C_max); AC = min(AC_raw, C_max).

F.2 Chronology scale (CC_raw)

Value Condition
4 Primary contemporaneous record fixes the date and a different lineage (R ≥ 2) corroborates it
3 Primary contemporaneous record fixes the date, or two independent lineages jointly fix it
2 Only a retrospective synthesis or single derivative account gives the date
1 No supporting source; undated, disputed, or inferred without a locator

F.3 Attribution scale (AC_raw, rebuilt)

Value Condition
4 As AC = 3, and the disconfirming test was run against at least one lineage outside the supporting set (adversarial test)
3 At least two independent lineages support the attribution, and the recorded disconfirming test has been run and passed
2 A single lineage supports it; or ≥ 2 lineages support it but the disconfirming test is untested or failed (§9.4 ceiling)
1 No supporting source; attribution rests on reputation or national label

Every value is now reachable: AC = 3 needs a passed test; AC = 4 an adversarial one. Because no disconfirming test has yet been run, no claim in the current dataset can exceed AC = 2. The first AC = 3 can appear only after Phase 4.

F.4 Impact evidence (IE)

0 = no outcome claim; 1 = capability exists (artifact, service, or policy in force); 2 = documented diffusion or access for a defined population; 3 = measured outcome for a defined population; 4 = causal study with counterfactual.

An announcement that a policy took effect is IE = 1, not 2. (v1.2 correction.)

F.5 The cap (single operative rule)

k_eff C_max Interpretation
0 1 Unsourced; Indeterminate = absence of support, not weak support
1 2 Single lineage; nothing above retrospective strength
≥ 2 4 Full range available

Equivalence note: v1.1 stated C_gen = min(4, 2+k) and C_anti separately. C_gen is never below C_anti at any k, so the lesser is always C_anti; the "joint cap" was one cap stated twice. v1.2 states it once. No v1.1 score changes because of the simplification.

F.6 Country-attribution rule (§8.4)

· AC_country = max(1, AC − 1) while the international-dependency field is untested.
· AC_country = AC once the dependency field has been tested.
· State-act exemption: where the bounded actor is the national government and the proposition is that government's own act (a program authorization, a policy decision), country and actor coincide, and AC_country = AC. The dependency question then bears on the effect of the act, which is an impact claim scored under §11, not on who performed it.
· Non-US / distributed / multinational claims: AC_country is not applicable.
· An AC_country of 1 produced by this rule alone, with AC > 1, is labeled dependency-untested (DTU), not Indeterminate: actor-level attribution is supported; the country-level step has not been tested.

v1.1 stated the cap but reported AC only, so the cap never appeared in any score. v1.2 reports AC_country explicitly.

F.7 Disconfirming-test rule (§9.4)

Every claim records at least one disconfirming test with status ∈ {untested, passed, passed-adversarial, failed}. Status feeds the attribution scale directly: an untested or failed test holds AC at 2. A claim cannot satisfy this rule by asserting that its own wording already encodes the test — the v1.1 treatment of US-NET-001 was circular on exactly this point.

